WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
Spark SQL and the DataFrame API are closely related.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:07.502
SQL expresses the logic with SQL syntax.

00:00:07.502 --> 00:00:11.882
The DataFrame API expresses transformations through code.

00:00:11.882 --> 00:00:15.922
Both can use Spark's structured execution engine.

00:00:15.922 --> 00:00:23.087
For students who already know SQL, Spark SQL is a natural entry point into distributed processing.

00:00:23.087 --> 00:00:28.387
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:33.057
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.057 --> 00:00:37.517
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:44.347
In a real setting such as a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions,

00:00:44.347 --> 00:00:50.717
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.967
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:59.411
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
